      "writtenOn": "2021-01-07T20:03:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "After giving the various paths more though, I think we want scrap the \"select_positive\" label and then do the following:\n\nif (points \u003d\u003d LONG_MIN)\n    goto next;\n\nif (task-\u003esignal-\u003eoom_score_adj \u003e\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 points \u003e oc-chosen_non_negative_adj_points) {\n    ....\n}\n\nif (points \u003c oc-\u003echosen_points)\n    goto next;\n\n\nLet\u0027s walk through my reasoning:\n\n1. Originally, I had placed the chosen_non_negative_adj tracking code (lines 375 - 380) to account for the case where a task satisfying oom_task_origin(task) is selected - however, that is redundant.  If the task has a positive ADJ, it will be contained in oc-\u003echosen after the iterations over the tasks have finished; if the task has a positive ADJ, there\u0027s no need for us to track it in oc-\u003echosen_non_negative_adj.  So, we can move lines 375 - 380 above the \"select:\" label.\n\n2. Now, the question is, should we move lines 375 - 380 inside of the block below?\n\nif (points \u003c oc-\u003echosen_points)\n    ....\n\nIf we did that, we could end up missing the worst task with a non-negative ADJ.  Let\u0027s say we\u0027re in iteration n of oom_evaluate_task(), and we encounter the task A with the highest badness score seen so far, such that the tasks ADJ score is negative.  On a subsequent iteration, the task B we\u0027re looking at could be the worst task with a non-negative ADJ - let\u0027s assume A\u0027s badness score is greater than B\u0027s.  Then if (points \u003c oc-\u003echosen_points) evaluates to false, and we won\u0027t end up setting oc-\u003echosen_non_negative_adj \u003d B;\n\nI\u0027ll be making the corresponding updates in the next patch.",
